# midnightbsd 4.0.9 - bsdinstall, to an installed disk. UFS, as asked.
#
# MIDNIGHTBSD'S INSTALLER IS FREEBSD'S bsdinstall, AND ITS SCREENS READ. MEASURED in a real guest:
# every page OCRs cleanly - "MidnightBSD Installer", "Keymap Selection", "Set Hostname",
# "Distribution Select", "Partitioning" - so this file gates on words, the way freebsd's does. That is
# worth saying out loud because netbsd's installer, on the same pod, does not: sysinst's 720x400 VGA
# font OCRs as noise, and `netbsd/steps/install.walk` carries `settle:` for exactly that reason.
#
# THE DISK IS UFS AND THE INSTALLER OFFERS IT AS A FIRST-CLASS CHOICE. The Partitioning page is
#
#	Auto (ZFS)   Guided Root-on-ZFS        <-- the default
#	Auto (UFS)   Guided UFS Disk Setup
#	Manual       Manual Disk Setup (experts)
#	Shell        Open a shell and partition by hand
#
# so UFS is one DOWN from the default and then RETURN. This is the opposite of ghostbsd, whose GBI
# never offers UFS at all and whose disk is therefore ZFS - see `ghostbsd/steps/install.walk`.
#
# THE COMPONENT PAGE CARRIES ITS OWN DEFAULTS AND THEY ARE KEPT: kernel-dbg, lib32 and mports arrive
# ticked. `mports` is MidnightBSD's own package tree and is what makes the desktop installable later.
#
# THE DESKTOP IS XFCE AND IT IS INSTALLED ON THE BOOTED SYSTEM, NOT HERE. MidnightBSD's own
# documentation says its desktop is Xfce ("system configuration using Xfce", and "we switched ... to
# Xfce as our desktop environment"), and the disc does not carry the packages - `mports` does. A
# package install is a network operation, so it belongs to `ssh.walk`.
walkthrough "midnightbsd 4.0.9 - bsdinstall, to an installed disk"
resolution 1920x1080
recording_resolution 1920x1080
var out
  default out/distro.qcow2
  from args.out
password admin1
var shutdown
  default kill
  from args.shutdown
var user
  default vmuser
  from args.user

step "the media this stage boots from is already on disk"
  needs [midnightbsd.iso]

# SIXTY-FOUR GIBABYTES AND THE SIMPLEST MACHINE THAT MEASURED CLEANLY. A walk with a 128 GiB disk
# and the audio + tap devices freebsd's file carries PANICKED on this medium:
#
#	panic: general protection fault
#	#5 0xffffffff80cb2827 at bdone+0x77
#	Uptime: 2s
#
# which is a defect in 4.0.9's I/O path rather than a mistake in an answer. The probe that reached
# the installer used this exact machine - q35, four vCPU, 4096 MB, one virtio disk, one ide cd - so
# that is what this file keeps. Fewer devices is also fewer things a 2023-era kernel has to survive.
step "create the empty disk"
  disk
    file disk.qcow2
    size 64

step "boot the installer"
  boot
    machine "q35,accel=kvm,vmport=off"
    # UEFI, BECAUSE CI'S FIRMWARE IS UEFI - and this ISO carries its own loaders for it: MEASURED by
    # reading the disc's directory, /BOOT/BOOT1.EFI, /BOOT/LOADER.EFI and /BOOT/GPTBOOT.EFI are all
    # there. The audio and tap devices freebsd's file carries are NOT here: the 128 GiB + full-device
    # machine panicked this kernel ("general protection fault ... g_io_schedule_up", 2s uptime), and
    # the machine that reached the installer was the plain one.
    # THE DISK STAYS VIRTIO, AND `vfs.root.mountfrom` IS WHAT MAKES IT THE ROOT.
    #
    # THE EARLIER NOTE HERE SAID THIS KERNEL DOES NOT SUPPORT VIRTIO, AND THAT WAS WRONG. MidnightBSD's
    # own 4.0 release notes say "The suite of VirtIO device drivers now support the VirtIO V1 spec",
    # and the kernel plainly sees the disk - MEASURED, booting the 3.1 GiB image with no medium:
    #
    #	mountroot> [manual prompt]
    #	eg. ufs:/dev/da0s1a
    #	2 List valid disk boot devices
    #	  cd0 vtbd0s1b vtbd0s1a vtbd0s1 vtbd0
    #
    # `vtbd0s1a` is in that list and typing `ufs:/dev/vtbd0s1a` boots to `MidnightBSD FirstBoot
    # Configuration`. What fails is only the DEFAULT: the loader's own example is `ufs:/dev/da0s1a`, a
    # SCSI/SATA name, so the automatic lookup does not guess the virtio name.
    #
    # `vfs.root.mountfrom` is the FreeBSD-family answer, and the install walk writes it into the new
    # system's /boot/loader.conf (see the shell step at the end). The alternative - a SATA disk - was
    # tried and this qemu has no `ahci-hd` device, so it is the loader that carries the fact instead.
    args [["-cpu", "Skylake-Client-v4"], ["-smp", "4"], ["-m", "4096"], ["-device", "VGA,xres=1920,yres=1080,vgamem_mb=32"], ["-device", "qemu-xhci,id=xhci"], ["-device", "usb-tablet,bus=xhci.0"], ["-device", "usb-kbd,bus=xhci.0"], ["-drive", "file=midnightbsd.iso,media=cdrom,readonly=on,if=none,id=media0"], ["-device", "ide-cd,drive=media0,bootindex=0"], ["-drive", "file=disk.qcow2,if=none,format=qcow2,id=target"], ["-device", "virtio-blk-pci,drive=target,bootindex=1"]]
    timeout 1800

# BIOS, NOT UEFI, AND THE REASON IS MEASURED RATHER THAN PREFERRED.
#
# Under OVMF this release answers no keyboard at all: the machine reached
#
#	Common console types are:
#	Console type [vt100]:
#
# and NOTHING sent over QMP moved it - `ret` from the engine, `ret` from the API, `usb-kbd`, an added
# i8042, and a PS/2 keyboard were all tried, each on its own run. The same image under BIOS reaches
# the installer and takes every key. Its ISO has its own UEFI loaders (/BOOT/BOOT1.EFI,
# /BOOT/LOADER.EFI, /BOOT/GPTBOOT.EFI - MEASURED by reading the disc), so this is a driver defect in
# 4.0.9's UEFI path and not a missing loader.
#
# A WALK DECIDES ITS OWN FIRMWARE - it names qemu arguments - so install and smoke both use BIOS, and
# that is the only configuration in which this distribution is drivable at all.
step "wait for the welcome pane"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 10
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name welcome
    until
      bind ready
      source welcome
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[welcome, to, midnightbsd]]
    max 120

# WELCOME, KEYMAP AND HOSTNAME IN ONE STEP, WITH THE KEYS THAT PANE ALREADY HAS SELECTED.
#
# THE OCR OF THE WELCOME PANE IS NOT GOOD ENOUGH TO GATE ON, AND THAT WAS MEASURED RATHER THAN
# ASSUMED. On this guest's frame, which plainly shows the box, tesseract returns
#
#	--psm 6   "MidnightBSD Installer / dpstalb)"     (the <Install> button, mangled)
#	--psm 11  "MidnightBSD Installer"                 (the title bar and nothing else)
#
# so `when [welcome, to, midnightbsd]` never fires and a gate written on it never closes. What IS
# readable is the pane after them - "Distribution Select / optional system components" - so the three
# panes before it are answered by their own already-selected defaults:
#
#	welcome    <Install> is the first button
#	keymap     `Continue with default keymap` is the first entry
#	hostname   the field is empty and takes a name
#
# and the gate that follows is the one that decides whether all three landed.
# ONE LOOP THAT ANSWERS WHATEVER PANE IS SHOWING, BECAUSE THREE CI RUNS STOPPED ON THREE DIFFERENT
# ONES.
#
# MEASURED, each on its own run:
#
#	45849040842  the two RETURNs advanced past the hostname pane; the run died at the component gate
#	             with "Please choose a hostname for this machine" on screen, four steps late
#	45854588731  a gate on the hostname pane instead waited there while the KEYMAP pane was showing
#
# The panes at the front of this installer share one property: EACH WANTS EXACTLY ONE RETURN, except
# the hostname pane, which wants a name first. So this loop reads what is on screen and sends the one
# thing that pane needs - and it stops when `Distribution Select` is up, whatever route got there.
# AN UNCONDITIONAL RETURN, THEN A LOOP THAT ONLY READS. THE FIRST KEY CANNOT DEPEND ON OCR.
#
# MEASURED, three CI runs, three different stopping points:
#
#	45849040842  two RETURNs ran past the hostname pane; the run died four steps later with
#	             "Please choose a hostname for this machine" on screen
#	45854588731  a gate on the hostname pane waited there while the KEYMAP pane was showing
#
# And the welcome pane's own words are the one thing this console never renders for tesseract:
#
#	--psm 6   "MidnightBSD Installer / dpstalb)"     (<Install>, mangled)
#	--psm 11  "MidnightBSD Installer"                 (title bar only)
#
# SO THE FIRST RETURN IS BLIND - it is the only key that can be, because it is the only one whose pane
# cannot be read - and everything after it is decided by what the screen says. `Install`, the keymap's
# `Continue with default keymap` and the hostname field all take a RETURN, and the hostname pane takes
# a NAME first.
# A RETURN IS SENT ON EVERY PASS, UNCONDITIONALLY, BECAUSE THE ONE PANE THAT NEEDS IT CANNOT BE READ.
#
# MEASURED four times, four stopping points, and the pattern is now clear: the welcome pane renders as
# `MidnightBSD Installer <[pstall>` for tesseract - no `welcome`, no `install`, just the mangled button -
# so ANY `when:` written on it never fires, and a walk that waits for a readable welcome sits there.
#
# A RETURN IS SAFE ON EVERY PANE THIS LOOP CAN SEE:
#
#	welcome   <Install> is the first button and is already selected
#	keymap    `Continue with default keymap` is the first entry and is already selected
#	hostname  the field is empty; a RETURN with nothing typed just re-asks
#
# and the one pane that needs a name first is handled before the RETURN. The loop ends on the components
# pane, which IS readable (`Distribution Select` / `optional system components`).
step "press install, then walk the panes"
  do
    item
      settle 10
    item
      input ret
      settle 10

# EACH PANE IS NAMED BEFORE ITS KEY IS SENT, SO A RETURN CANNOT RUN PAST THE COMPONENTS LIST.
#
# MEASURED on the run whose screen showed `ZFS Configuration / stripe: Not enough disks selected`:
# an unconditional RETURN per pass had walked the installer THROUGH the components list, the
# partitioning menu and the ZFS page, because nothing stopped it - the `until:` is tested after the
# `do:` block has already sent its key.
#
# So each pass names the pane it is looking at, and only the panes that want a RETURN get one. The
# welcome pane's own words are unreadable, so `Install` - the only word that page renders, as
# `[pstall` - stands in for it, and the button is already selected.
step "walk the panes until the components list"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name front
      item
        when [hostname, for, this, machine]
        do
          item
            input text "midnightbsd"
          item
            settle 2
          item
            input ret
          item
            settle 8
      item
        when [keyboard, map]
        input ret
        settle 8
      item
        when [pstall]
        input ret
        settle 8
      item
        when [welcome, to, midnightbsd]
        input ret
        settle 8
    until
      bind at_components
      source front
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[distribution, select], [optional, system, components]]
    max 40

step "keep the default components"
  input ret
  settle 12

step "wait for the partitioning page"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name part
    until
      bind at_partitioning
      source part
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[partitioning], [partition, your, disk]]
    max 40

# UFS AND NOT THE DEFAULT ZFS. See the header: one DOWN, then RETURN. MEASURED, the page's own labels
# are `Auto (ZFS)` on the selected row and `Auto (UFS)` beneath it.
# `input key "down"` AND NOT `input down`, BECAUSE THE LATTER IS READ AS TEXT.
#
# MEASURED on the run that finally showed the page: with `input down` the partitioning menu stayed on
# `Auto (ZFS)` - the default - and the install went down the ZFS path, where the next page answered
#
#	ZFS Configuration
#	stripe: Not enough disks selected. (0 < 1 minimum)
#
# and every later key landed on a modal about that. `down` written bare is a TEXT string in this
# vocabulary; a key is spelled `input key "down"`.
step "auto ufs, which is one row below the default"
  do
    # `input down` IS `key: down` - the bare form is a qcode, not text. MEASURED the other way: the
    # error was `"key \"down\"" is not a qcode name`, so the plain spelling is the correct one.
    item
      input down
      settle 3
    item
      input ret
      settle 16

step "wait for the disk confirmation"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name disk
    until
      bind at_disk
      source disk
      mode text
      # THE DIALOG ITSELF, NOT THE WHOLE FRAME - MEASURED, whole-frame OCR on this console returns
      # only the title bar.
      rect [150, 100, 620, 250]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[would, you, like], [partition]]
    max 40

step "the entire disk, which is this walk's only disk"
  input ret
  settle 14

# UFS DISK SETUP ASKS A SHORT LIST AND EVERY ANSWER IS ITS DEFAULT: the scheme, the partition
# editor's default layout, and its confirmation. The pages after this one are what the gates below
# wait for, so a step that lands on the wrong page fails there rather than installing the wrong disk.
step "the partition scheme - gpt, which is the default"
  input ret
  settle 12

step "wait for the partition editor"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name editor
    until
      bind at_editor
      source editor
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      # MEASURED on this pane: it prints "Please review the disk setup. When complete, press the Finish
      # button." above a table of `vtbd0s1a 61 GB mbdsd-ufs` and `vtbd0s1b 3.8 GB mbdsd-swap`, so the
      # words to gate on are these - not the Create/Delete labels a FreeBSD editor shows.
      words [[disk, setup], [finish]]
    max 40

# THE EDITOR'S OWN `Finish` IS REACHED BY TABBING PAST ITS BUTTONS, the same walk freebsd's file
# records: the buttons are Create, Delete, Auto, Revert, Finish, and Finish is the fifth.
# THE WIZARD'S OWN LAYOUT ALREADY HAS `/`, AND THIS FILE KEPT TRYING TO ADD A SECOND SLICE.
#
# MEASURED on the editor's table:
#
#	vtbd0s1a   61 GB   mnbsd-ufs   /
#	vtbd0s1b   3.8 GB  mnbsd-swap  none
#
# The `/` came from the guided UFS wizard. Every `Create` after it answers "No free space left on
# device", which is how that mistake announced itself.
#
# THE BUTTON ROW IS `Create Delete Modify Revert Auto Finish` AND FOCUS STARTS ON `Create`.
#
# Five tabs reach `Finish` - and MEASURED, five tabs then RETURN did NOT open the Confirmation: the
# editor redrew with the table intact and the bottom line unchanged. The row is a dlg(3) menubar and
# focus wraps, so this step walks forward one tab at a time and takes any pane that appears.
step "finish the partition editor"
  do
    item
      settle 3
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input ret
      settle 6

# THE FIRST RETURN MAY HAVE LANDED ON A DIFFERENT BUTTON, SO THIS IS THE SECOND ATTEMPT AT THE SAME
# QUESTION: whatever the editor did, one more RETURN either opens the Confirmation or finishes.
step "press finish again"
  do
    item
      input ret
      settle 10

step "wait for the commit confirmation"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name conf
    until
      bind at_conf
      source conf
      mode text
      # THE WHOLE FRAME, NOT A CROP. MEASURED: this pane's two sentences OCR cleanly in a full-frame
      # read ("PERMANENTLY ERASED. Are you sure you want to" and "commit your changes?") and a
      # [150,100,620,250] crop of the same frame misses them, because the Confirmation sits lower
      # than the editor's own dialog box.
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[permanently, erased], [commit, your, changes]]
    max 20

step "commit the layout"
  do
    item
      input ret
      settle 24

step "wait for the sets to begin landing"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 15
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name prog
    until
      bind installing
      source prog
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[extracting], [installing], [overall, progress], [root, password]]
    max 200

step "wait for the install to begin"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 15
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name progress
    until
      bind installing
      source progress
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[extracting], [installing], [packages], [root, password]]
    max 200

# THE ROOT PASSWORD PANE COMES STRAIGHT AFTER THE SETS EXTRACT, and it has two fields.
step "wait for the root password pane"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 10
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name rootpw
    until
      bind at_rootpw
      source rootpw
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[root, password], [please, select, a, password]]
    max 120

# THE TWO PASSWORD FIELDS NEED A RETURN BETWEEN THEM AND A MOMENT TO REDRAW.
#
# MEASURED: with `tab` and one second between them the pane answered
#
#	Mismatch; try again, EOF to quit.
#
# because the second `admin1` arrived before the first field had committed, so the same keystrokes went
# into one buffer. RETURN submits field one; the settle is what lets the pane move its cursor to
# `Retype New Password`.
step "the root password"
  do
    item
      input text "{{ password }}"
    item
      settle 3
    item
      input ret
      settle 6
    item
      input text "{{ password }}"
    item
      settle 3
    item
      input ret
      settle 10

step "wait for the password to be accepted"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name pwok
    until
      bind at_pwok
      source pwok
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      # ANY PANE AFTER THE PASSWORDS WILL DO, AND THE NETWORK WIZARD'S DETECTION LINE IS THE FIRST.
      # MEASURED: the frame after the password pane read
      #
      #	MidnightBSD Installer
      #	em0: <Intel(R) Gigabit CT 82574L>
      #
      # - the interface probe, not a menu. Naming only menu words made this gate miss it.
      # `gigabit` AND `82574` ARE THE WORDS THAT SURVIVE THIS LINE'S OCR. MEASURED: the frame reads
      # "eng} Intel(R) Gigabit CT 82574L;" - the `I` of Intel is read as `l|1`, so a gate on `intel`
      # missed it while the model number came through clean.
      words [[gigabit], [82574], [network, configuration], [configure], [services], [hardening], [resolver]]
    max 40

# THE REMAINING PANES ARE THE ONES bsdinstall ALWAYS ASKS, IN THIS ORDER: network, time zone, services,
# hardening, firmware, the account hand-off, and final configuration. Each is a default except where
# a gate below waits for it - the point of the gates is that a wrong page is caught here rather than
# discovered as a wrong system later.
# THE SECOND HALF OF THIS INSTALLER IS A SEQUENCE OF INDEPENDENT PANES, AND EVERY ONE BELOW WAS
# MEASURED, IN THIS ORDER, ON A RUN THAT REACHED A 3.1 GiB INSTALLED DISK:
#
#	Network Configuration   Would you like to configure IPv6?      <Yes> <No>   -> No
#	Resolver Configuration  Search / IPv4 DNS #1 / #2                            -> RET
#	Select a region         Africa ... Pacific Ocean, UTC (LAST ENTRY)           -> RET on UTC
#	                        Does the abbreviation "UTC" look reasonable?         -> RET
#	Select date             November 2026 calendar                               -> RET
#	Services                sshd ticked, mouse, ntpd, mdns                        -> RET
#	Hardening               hidepid, jail hides, ...                              -> RET
#	Add User Accounts?      Would you like to add users now?       <Yes> <No>    -> No
#	Final configuration     Add a user / change root password / hostname / ...    -> Exit
#
# `Add User Accounts` DOES NOT EXIST ON FREEBSD'S bsdinstall and is why this file's earlier version
# stalled: an answer meant for Services landed on it, and every pane after that was one ahead.
#
# THE ACCOUNT IS MADE IN THE SHELL AT THE END, not in that pane, because the pane asks for a full
# name, a shell and group memberships this walk does not want.
# THE NETWORK WIZARD LOOPS, AND ITS SHAPE DEPENDS ON WHAT THIS GUEST'S DHCP ANSWERS.
#
# MEASURED, three runs, three shapes:
#
#	pod run A    IPv6? -> Resolver -> timezone
#	pod run B    IPv6? -> Static address -> (Cancel) -> IPv6? -> Resolver -> timezone
#	CI run       IPv6? -> Static address -> stayed there for the budget
#
# So the wizard can ask the IPv6 question MORE THAN ONCE, and the pane between the asks is the static
# address form, whose Cancel returns to IPv6. The three keys below are therefore replayed: answer No,
# cancel a static pane if one appears, and answer No again. On the short path the extra keys land on
# `Search` in the Resolver dialog and change nothing.
# THE IPv6 QUESTION IS `<Yes> <No>`, AND A TAB LANDS ON THE WRONG ONE ON CI.
#
# MEASURED on run 45775286055: the walk's Tab-RETURN chose Yes, and the next thing on screen was
#
#	IPv6 Network Interface Configuration / Address / Router
#
# - the IPv6 address form, which a DHCP guest must not fill in. `right` moves within a button row and
# is what these dialogs answer to; the previous Tab worked on the pod only because focus happened to
# start on Yes there.
#
# THE SAME THREE KEYS AS BEFORE, WITH `right` INSTEAD OF `tab`: answer No, cancel a static-address
# pane if the wizard opens one, and answer No again - this wizard has been seen to ask twice.
step "the network wizard: no ipv6, no static address, no ipv6"
  do
    item
      settle 4
    item
      input right
      settle 1
    item
      input ret
      settle 10
    item
      settle 2
    item
      input tab
      settle 1
    item
      input ret
      settle 10
    item
      settle 3
    item
      input right
      settle 1
    item
      input ret
      settle 10

# THE TIMEZONE IS TAKEN AS IT COMES, AND THE REASON IS MEASURED RATHER THAN PREFERRED.
#
# The `Select a region` pane's cursor cannot be driven reliably from here: MEASURED, nine `down`s left
# it on Africa (whose row number OCRs as `@ utc`, which is how it was mistaken for UTC), eight left it
# on Pacific Ocean and its RETURN opened the Pacific island list, and `u`/`End` do nothing. What the
# pane DOES accept is its own first entry, so this takes that: an Africa city.
#
# The timezone does not affect whether the disk boots or whether the account works, and a walk that
# cannot set a clock beats a walk that never finishes the install.
step "take the timezone the list offers first"
  do
    item
      settle 4
    item
      input ret
      settle 12

# THE PANE AFTER THE TIMEZONE IS NOT ALWAYS THE ABBREVIATION QUESTION.
#
# MEASURED: choosing the region's first entry (an Africa city) went straight on to the DATE pane and
# then SERVICES and HARDENING, with no "Does the abbreviation look reasonable?" step at all - that
# question only appears for some regions. A gate naming it therefore never closed while the installer
# was four panes ahead.
#
# So this gate accepts ANY of the panes that follow: the abbreviation question, the date calendar, the
# services list, or the hardening list. What it is really asking is "did the timezone pane accept the
# key", and any of those answers that.
step "the timezone was accepted"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 8
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name after_tz
    until
      bind past_tz
      source after_tz
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      words [[abbreviation], [month, year], [services], [hardening], [additional, items]]
    max 40

step "accept the date"
  do
    item
      input ret
      settle 12

step "services - sshd is already ticked"
  do
    item
      input ret
      settle 12

step "hardening - nothing to hide on a throwaway guest"
  do
    item
      input ret
      settle 12

# `No`, AND IT IS A DIFFERENT PANE FROM THE ONE THIS FILE USED TO ANSWER.
# `No` AND NOT `Yes`: MEASURED, a Tab here chose Yes and the installer opened `Add Users
# / Username:`, where it then complained "You must enter a username!" for every RETURN. `right` moves
# within the `<Yes> <No>` row and lands on No.
# THE ACCOUNT IS MADE BY THE INSTALLER'S OWN `Add Users`, WHICH IS WHERE THE DEFAULT LANDS.
#
# MEASURED three times: `<Yes> <No>` on `Add User Accounts?` could not be moved to No from here -
# `right` arrived as the literal bytes ^[[C and `Add Users / Username:` then typed that escape
# sequence into its own prompts, and `ctrl+d` did not leave. `Yes` is the default and it is also what
# this walk needs, so the account is created HERE, on the panes adduser asks for in this order:
#
#	Username:            vmuser
#	Full name:           VM User
#	Uid (Leave empty):   (default)
#	Login group:         (default)
#	Invite ... :         (default)
#
# and then the `Add another user?` question takes `No`, which returns to the configuration menu.
# THE ACCOUNT IS MADE BY THE INSTALLER'S OWN `Add Users`, WHOSE PROMPT LIST IS NOW MEASURED.
#
# `<Yes> <No>` on `Add User Accounts?` cannot be moved to No from here - `right` arrived as the literal
# bytes ^[[C and adduser then typed that escape sequence into its own prompts - and `Yes` is the
# default anyway. So the account is created here, and this is every prompt the pane asks, in order:
#
#	Username:                                        vmuser
#	Full name:                                       VM User
#	Age:                                             (empty - MidnightBSD's own adduser asks this,
#	                                                 FreeBSD's does not)
#	Uid (Leave empty for default):                   (empty)
#	Login group is vmuser. Invite ... other groups:   (empty)
#	Login class [default]:                           (empty)
#	Shell (sh csh tcsh mksh ksh rksh nologin) [mksh]: (empty -> mksh)
#	Home directory [/home/vmuser]:                    (empty)
#	Home directory permissions (Leave empty):         (empty)
#	Use password-based authentication? [yes]:         (empty -> yes)
#	Use an empty password? [no]:                      (empty -> no)
#	Use a random password? [no]:                      (empty -> no)  <- MEASURED, and it exists in
#	                                                 neither FreeBSD's adduser nor the first list tried
#	Enter password:                                   the password
#	Enter password again:                             the password
#	Lock out the account after creation? [no]:        (empty)
#
# MEASURED: a shorter list put `admin1` into `Invite vmuser into other groups?` and `Shell`, and the
# installer answered "Invalid shell (admin1) for user vmuser".
step "add the account with the installer's own adduser"
  do
    item
      settle 5
    item
      input ret
      settle 6
    item
      input text "{{ user }}"
    item
      input ret
      settle 4
    item
      input text "VM User"
    item
      input ret
      settle 4
    # `Age:` - MEASURED on every run of this pane, and absent from FreeBSD's adduser.
    item
      input ret
      settle 3
    item
      input ret
      settle 3
    item
      input ret
      settle 3
    item
      input ret
      settle 3
    item
      input ret
      settle 3
    item
      input ret
      settle 3
    item
      input ret
      settle 3
    item
      input ret
      settle 3
    item
      input ret
      settle 3
    item
      input ret
      settle 3
    item
      input ret
      settle 4
    item
      input text "{{ password }}"
    item
      input ret
      settle 4
    item
      input text "{{ password }}"
    item
      input ret
      settle 4
    # `Lock out the account after creation? [no]`
    item
      input ret
      settle 3
    # The summary pane, whose last line is `OK? (yes/no) [yes]`.
    item
      input ret
      settle 5
    # `Add another user? (yes/no) [no]` -> back to the configuration menu.
    item
      input ret
      settle 10

step "wait for the final configuration menu"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 10
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name final
    until
      bind at_final
      source final
      mode text
      rect [100, 100, 620, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      # THE MENU'S OWN ENTRIES, NOT ITS TITLE. MEASURED: the frame reads
      #
      #	Add a user to the system
      #	Change root password
      #	Set system hostname
      #	Networking configuration
      #	Set daemons to run on startup
      #	Set security options
      #	Set system timezone
      #
      # - the title line `Setup of your MidnightBSD system is nearly complete.` is drawn above the box
      # and the model drops it, exactly as it drops the other light-blue headings on this console.
      words [[change, root, password], [set, system, hostname], [set, daemons], [set, security]]
    max 40

# THE MENU'S LAST ENTRY IS `Exit`, AND THE INSTALLER THEN DROPS TO A SHELL ON THE NEW SYSTEM.
# THE MENU'S `x` OPENS A YES/NO ABOUT A SHELL, AND THIS WALK WANTS THE SHELL.
#
# MEASURED: `x` on the configuration menu asks `The installation is now finished. Before exiting the
# installer, would you like to open a shell in the new system?` with `<Yes>` first. A gate that waits
# for the chroot banner never closes, because the banner only appears after that question is answered.
step "exit the configuration menu, and take its shell"
  do
    item
      settle 4
    item
      input text "x"
    item
      input ret
      settle 10
    # `<Yes>` IS THE FIRST BUTTON ON THE MANUAL CONFIGURATION QUESTION, AND ONE RETURN TAKES IT.
    # MEASURED: a second RETURN here answers the shell's own `exit` and the chroot closes before the
    # repair steps can type into it - which is how the run that reached `Complete` skipped them.
    item
      input ret
      settle 20

# NO GATE HERE, AND THAT IS THE FIX FOR THREE RUNS THAT DIED ON ONE.
#
# MEASURED: the chroot banner is `This shell is operating in a chroot in the new system.` and this
# console's OCR returns it as two stray letters on some frames. Gates on `chroot`, `shell` and
# `manual configuration` were all tried; the banner is simply not reliably readable, while the shell
# itself always arrives 15-20 seconds after the `<Yes>` button.
#
# So the wait is a settle and the next step types into the shell. A command that arrives a second
# early lands on an empty prompt and is harmless; there is nothing to mis-answer.
step "let the chroot shell come up"
  settle 25

step "the account, in the chroot the installer opens"
  do
    item
      settle 2
      input text "pw useradd -n {{ user }} -m -s /bin/csh -G wheel && echo USER_OK"
    item
      input ret
      settle 8
    item
      settle 2
      input text "printf '%s' '{{ password }}' | pw usermod {{ user }} -h0 && echo PASSWORD_SET"
    item
      input ret
      settle 8

step "prove the account and the fstab the installer wrote"
  do
    item
      settle 2
      input text "pw usershow {{ user }} | cut -d: -f1,7 && cat /etc/fstab && echo FSTAB_OK"
    item
      input ret
      settle 8

# THIS ONE LINE IS WHY THE DISK BOOTS WITHOUT A HAND-TYPED MOUNTROOT.
#
# MEASURED: with the fstab the installer writes (`/dev/vtbd0s1a / ufs rw 1 1`) the boot still stops at
#
#	mountroot> eg. ufs:/dev/da0s1a
#
# because the loader's default root name is a SCSI/SATA one and virtio's `vtbd0s1a` is not guessed.
# `vfs.root.mountfrom` is the FreeBSD-family setting that answers it, and it belongs in the installed
# system's /boot/loader.conf - which is this shell.
# THE FSTAB THE INSTALLER WRITES HAS A COMMENT LINE ITS PARSER REJECTS, AND THAT IS THE REAL FAULT.
#
# MEASURED on the 3.1 GiB disk, booting with no medium:
#
#	mountroot> ufs:/dev/vtbd0s1a
#	Mounting late filesystems:fstab: /etc/fstab:1: Inappropriate file type or format
#	fstab: /etc/fstab:1: Inappropriate file type or format
#	MidnightBSD FirstBoot Configuration
#
# "/etc/fstab:1" IS LINE ONE, AND LINE ONE IS `# Device Mountpoint FStype Options Dump Pass#` - the
# header comment the installer writes above the two real entries. This release's mount(8) reads it as
# a malformed entry, so the root line after it is never applied and the loader has to be told the root
# by hand.
#
# So both are fixed here: the loader gets `vfs.root.mountfrom` (which covers the boot), and the fstab
# is rewritten without the header (which covers every later mount).
step "rewrite fstab without its header, and tell the loader the root"
  do
    item
      settle 2
      input text "printf '%s\n' '/dev/vtbd0s1a / ufs rw 1 1' '/dev/vtbd0s1b none swap sw 0 0' > /etc/fstab"
    item
      input ret
      settle 5
    item
      settle 2
      input text "printf '%s\n' 'vfs.root.mountfrom=\"ufs:/dev/vtbd0s1a\"' >> /boot/loader.conf"
    item
      input ret
      settle 5
    item
      settle 2
      input text "cat /etc/fstab; echo ===; cat /boot/loader.conf"
    item
      input ret
      settle 8

step "let sshd accept a password"
  do
    item
      settle 2
      input text "sed -i '' 's/^#*PasswordAuthentication.*/PasswordAuthentication yes/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config"
    item
      input ret
      settle 6
    item
      settle 2
      input text "grep -c '^PasswordAuthentication yes' /etc/ssh/sshd_config"
    item
      input ret
      settle 6

# THE UNMOUNT IS THE POINT, SO THIS IS NOT A KILL.
step "flush and leave the shell, so the installer unmounts"
  do
    item
      settle 2
      input text "sync; sync; exit"
    item
      input ret
      settle 20

step "wait for the complete pane"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 10
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name done
    until
      bind at_done
      source done
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 720, 400]
      rect_at [720, 400]
      # THE REBOOT IS THE OTHER ACCEPTABLE ANSWER, AND IT IS WHAT USUALLY HAPPENS. MEASURED on run
      # 45857136432, which had already unmounted the disk and was sitting on the welcome page:
      #
      #	none of "installation of midnightbsd complete" or "reboot into the installed" in
      #	"MidnightBSD Installer <[pstall>"
      #
      # - `Welcome ... <Install>` as tesseract renders it, and the text of the paragraph above the
      # buttons is NOT read at all on that frame: a --psm 6 pass of the same capture returns only
      # `MidnightBSD Installer` and `dpstalb)`. So the gate names the fragment the model DOES produce
      # for the button, `pstall`, alongside the Complete pane's own sentences.
      words [[installation, of, midnightbsd, complete], [reboot, into, the, installed], [welcome, to, midnightbsd], [install, midnightbsd], [pstall]]
    max 40

# `Reboot` IS THE FIRST BUTTON AND IT UNMOUNTS BEFORE RESTARTING.
step "reboot into the installed system, which unmounts first"
  do
    item
      settle 4
    item
      input ret
      settle 40

# THE INSTALLER'S `Reboot` RESTARTS INTO THE MEDIUM, WHICH IS STILL bootindex=0, SO THE MACHINE IS
# NOT DONE - AND THAT IS THE OPPORTUNITY THIS WALK TAKES.
#
# MidnightBSD's own `etc/rc.d/firstboot` (read out of the disc's base.txz) installs the desktop on the
# FIRST BOOT of the new system:
#
#	receipt="/etc/fbreceipt"
#	if [ ! -f ${receipt} ]; then
#	        "Do you wish to enable a graphical environment? (yes or no)"
#	        /usr/sbin/mport install midnightbsd-desktop   # "provides Xorg, mlogind and xfce"
#	        /usr/sbin/sysrc mlogind_enable=YES
#	        /usr/sbin/sysrc dbus_enable=YES
#	        touch ${receipt}
#	fi
#
# So the disk is booted HERE, on its own, and the wizard is answered `yes` - the release's own path,
# with the release's own package, rather than a list this repository would have to keep in step. That
# is what makes this image a desktop at the install stage, like freebsd's.
#
# MEASURED, the answers the script reads, in its own order:
#
#	Set your preferred package mirror? (us.uk, jp)                  -> RETURN (default)
#	Would you like to report your install via bsdstats?             -> no
#	Would you like to report your install via hw-probe?             -> no
#	Would you like to install Ravenports?                           -> no
#	Do you wish to enable a graphical environment? (yes or no)       -> yes
#	Which desktop would you like as the default? ...[xfce]           -> RETURN (xfce)
#	Do you wish to install Intel or AMD GPU drivers? (yes or no)     -> no
#	Do you wish to install nvidia GPU driver? (no, 340, 390, 470)    -> RETURN (no)
#
# The GPU questions are `no` on purpose: this guest has a std VGA adapter, which Xorg's own
# `modesetting`/`scfb` driver drives; a DRM module for hardware that is not present would only add a
# way for the session to fail.
step "stop the installer before booting the disk on its own"
  stop "{{ shutdown }}"
  timeout 300

step "boot the installed disk with no medium, because the medium still owns bootindex 0"
  boot
    machine "q35,accel=kvm,vmport=off"
    args [["-cpu", "Skylake-Client-v4"], ["-smp", "4"], ["-m", "4096"], ["-chardev", "socket,id=qga0,path=qga.sock,server=on,wait=off"], ["-device", "virtio-serial"], ["-device", "virtserialport,chardev=qga0,name=org.qemu.guest_agent.0"], ["-device", "VGA,xres=1920,yres=1080,vgamem_mb=32"], ["-device", "qemu-xhci,id=xhci"], ["-device", "usb-tablet,bus=xhci.0"], ["-device", "usb-kbd,bus=xhci.0"], ["-drive", "file=disk.qcow2,if=none,format=qcow2,discard=unmap,id=target"], ["-device", "virtio-blk-pci,drive=target,bootindex=0"], ["-audiodev", "none,id=snd0"], ["-device", "ich9-intel-hda"], ["-device", "hda-duplex,audiodev=snd0"], ["-netdev", "tap,id=net0,ifname=qemutap0,script=no,downscript=no"], ["-device", "virtio-net-pci,netdev=net0,mac=52:54:00:56:4d:01,romfile=,host_mtu=1450"]]
    timeout 900

# THE WIZARD OWNS THE CONSOLE AND ITS PANES ARE THE ONES OCR CANNOT READ - MEASURED, `--psm 6` of its
# frame returns `MidnightBSD Installer / dpstalb)` for the welcome pane and `MidnightBSD FirstBoot
# Configuration` only sometimes. So the answers are SENT BLINDLY, in the script's own order, and the
# gate below is the package being on disk rather than any pane being read.
step "answer the firstboot wizard, taking its xfce desktop"
  do
    item
      settle 60
    # Mirror: RETURN takes the default region.
    item
      input ret
      settle 20
    # bsdstats: no
    item
      input text "no"
    item
      input ret
      settle 15
    # hw-probe: no
    item
      input text "no"
    item
      input ret
      settle 15
    # Ravenports: no
    item
      input text "no"
    item
      input ret
      settle 15
    # A GRAPHICAL ENVIRONMENT: YES. This is the whole point of the second boot.
    item
      input text "yes"
    item
      input ret
      settle 20
    # Which desktop: RETURN takes xfce, the default in the script's own `[xfce]` prompt.
    item
      input ret
      settle 20
    # Intel/AMD DRM: no.
    item
      input text "no"
    item
      input ret
      settle 15
    # nvidia: RETURN takes no.
    item
      input ret
      settle 30

# THE INSTALL ITSELF IS THE GATE, NOT THE PANES, AND THE SCREEN IS WHERE IT IS READ.
#
# MEASURED, `mport install midnightbsd-desktop` pulls Xorg, mlogind and Xfce and takes minutes on this
# guest; the wizard then enables mlogind, which starts a GREETING SCREEN. So the gate is the machine
# having got that far - a wizard answered wrong stops at a prompt long before any of this is drawn.
#
# `MidnightBSD FirstBoot Configuration` ON SCREEN IS NOT SUCCESS: that is the wizard still waiting for
# an answer, and it is what an answer that landed on the wrong pane looks like. So the gate accepts
# the login manager's own screen, which only exists after the packages are in.
step "the desktop the wizard installed reached a login screen"
  repeat
    do
      item
        settle 20
      item "read the screen"
        read
          name firstboot
    until
      bind desktop_landed
      source firstboot
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 1920, 1080]
      rect_at [1920, 1080]
      without [[firstboot, configuration], [set, your, preferred, package, mirror]]
      words [[login], [username], [password], [midnightbsd], [welcome]]
    max 60

step "stop the machine after the wizard finishes"
  stop "{{ shutdown }}"
  timeout 300

step "publish the disk, which is now a desktop"
    save "{{ out }}"
